### Detailed Pharmacological Mechanisms

#### 1. The Asava/Arishta Disulfiram-Ethanol Shock Cascade
* **Biochemical Mechanism:** Classical formulations like *Draksharishta*, *Dashmoolarishta*, and *Amritarishta* rely on natural fermentation driven by *Woodfordia fruticosa* (Dhataki flowers), yielding an inherent ethanol concentration of **5% to 12% v/v**.
* **Clinical Hazard:** Nitroimidazoles (Metronidazole) selectively inhibit hepatic **aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH)**.
  $$\text{Ethanol} \xrightarrow{\text{ADH}} \text{Acetaldehyde} \xrightarrow[\mathbf{INHIBITED}]{\text{ALDH}} \text{Acetate}$$
  Within 10 minutes of taking 20 mL of *Draksharishta* alongside Metronidazole, acetaldehyde levels spike 10-fold. The patient develops intense flushing, throbbing headache, respiratory constriction, chest pain, and severe hypotension ($<75/40\text{ mmHg}$).

#### 2. The Licorice (*Yashtimadhu*) Pseudo-Aldosteronism Trap
* **Biochemical Mechanism:** Glycyrrhizic acid metabolite glycyrrhetinic acid inhibits **$11\beta$-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 ($11\beta$-HSD2)**:
  $$\text{Active Cortisol} \mathrel{\mathop{\cancel{\longrightarrow}}^{\mathbf{11\beta\text{-HSD2}}}} \text{Inactive Cortisone}$$
* **Clinical Hazard:** Uninactivated cortisol binds renal mineralocorticoid receptors with high affinity, activating the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) and dumping potassium into the urine.
  * In a patient on **Furosemide (Lasix)**, potassium drops to $< 2.2\text{ mEq/L}$.
  * In a cardiac patient on **Digoxin**, hypokalemia enhances myocardial sensitivity to digitalis, precipitating lethal **Ventricular Fibrillation**.

